// Peak/off-peak PTU-vs-PAYG recommendation report. Read-only reporting —
// see guides/advanced-ptu-routing.md in the accelerator fork for why this
// stops at a recommendation a human acts on, rather than automated
// routing.
//
// REQUIRES, in this order: 10-heatmap-moving-average.dax (Hour Of Day,
// Date Only columns), 06-ptu-allocation-columns.m (ptuWeightTokens,
// billingMonth), 08-payg-ptu-breakeven-column.m (paygEquivalentCost),
// 01-measures.dax's PTU section ([PTU Allocated Cost] etc).

// ============================================================
// PEAK-HOUR CLASSIFICATION (calculated column — depends on Hour Of Day
// from 10-heatmap-moving-average.dax)
// ============================================================

Is Peak Hour =
VAR ThisHourAvgTokens =
    CALCULATE(
        DIVIDE(SUM('llm-usage-container'[totalTokens]), DISTINCTCOUNT('llm-usage-container'[Date Only])),
        ALLEXCEPT('llm-usage-container', 'llm-usage-container'[Hour Of Day])
    )
VAR OverallAvgTokensPerHour =
    CALCULATE(
        DIVIDE(SUM('llm-usage-container'[totalTokens]), DISTINCTCOUNT('llm-usage-container'[Date Only]) * 24)
    )
RETURN
IF(DIVIDE(ThisHourAvgTokens, OverallAvgTokensPerHour) > 1.2, "Peak", "Off-Peak")
// A hour is "Peak" if its average token volume runs >20% above the
// all-hours average — a simple, explainable, adjustable rule (change the
// 1.2 threshold) rather than a fixed top-N-hours cutoff. Computed as a
// calculated column so it can be used directly as a CALCULATE filter
// below; only 24 distinct Hour Of Day values exist, so the ALLEXCEPT
// recomputation this involves is cheap regardless of table size.

// ============================================================
// PEAK VS. OFF-PEAK ECONOMICS, PER PTU DEPLOYMENT
// ============================================================

Off-Peak Weight Share =
DIVIDE(
    CALCULATE(SUM('llm-usage-container'[ptuWeightTokens]), 'llm-usage-container'[Is Peak Hour] = "Off-Peak"),
    SUM('llm-usage-container'[ptuWeightTokens])
)
// What fraction of this deployment's PTU-weighted usage happens during
// off-peak hours — the headroom that's sitting there unused relative to
// what the deployment was sized for at peak.

Off-Peak Allocated PTU Cost = [PTU Allocated Cost] * [Off-Peak Weight Share]
// This deployment's fixed monthly cost, apportioned to off-peak hours by
// their share of usage (not their share of the fixed cost — the fixed
// cost doesn't shrink off-peak, that's exactly the point being measured).

Off-Peak PAYG Equivalent Cost =
CALCULATE([PAYG Equivalent Cost], 'llm-usage-container'[Is Peak Hour] = "Off-Peak")
// What the off-peak-hour requests alone would have cost on PAYG.

Estimated Off-Peak Overpay =
[Off-Peak Allocated PTU Cost] - [Off-Peak PAYG Equivalent Cost]
// Positive = during off-peak hours, this deployment's share of the fixed
// PTU cost exceeds what those same requests would have cost on PAYG —
// the quantified case for "PTU at peak, PAYG off-peak."
//
// IMPORTANT CAVEAT, read before presenting this number: this is a
// THEORETICAL upper bound, not money you can recover today. Most PTU
// reservations are a fixed monthly commitment that can't be shed
// hour-by-hour — this measures the opportunity a dynamic-routing
// capability (guides/advanced-ptu-routing.md — explicitly NOT built) would
// unlock, not a saving available by changing a Power BI filter. Label it
// as such on the visual (e.g. "Estimated off-peak overpay (if capacity
// could flex hourly)"), don't present it as a number the business can
// bank without that feature existing first.

Peak-Hour Utilization =
DIVIDE(
    CALCULATE(SUM('llm-usage-container'[ptuWeightTokens]), 'llm-usage-container'[Is Peak Hour] = "Peak"),
    [PTU Deployment Monthly Weight Total]
)
// The flip side of Off-Peak Weight Share — how much of this deployment's
// total monthly weighted usage is concentrated in peak hours. High peak
// concentration + high Estimated Off-Peak Overpay together are the
// strongest case for pursuing the dynamic-routing feature; low overpay
// means the reservation is already well-utilized around the clock and
// there's little to gain from complicating routing.
